tls (Secure Transport): a certificate the caller trusts by itself, and which one was turned down

A media server on a home network shows a certificate nothing vouches for
but itself. The handshake fails, as it should for a stranger. A person
who has been shown that certificate and said "this is my server" has no
way to be heard: the choices were to verify every certificate against
the system's roots or to verify none.

With the `trusted_cert_sha256` option a caller names one certificate by
the SHA-256 of it as it is sent, in hexadecimal, colons or not. A peer
that shows exactly that certificate is taken as it is, whatever else is
the matter with it (no root, run out, written for another name): the
peer holds its key, and that certificate is what was trusted. Any other
certificate is judged as it was before, so a server with a certificate
the system does trust still connects, and a different one that nothing
vouches for is still turned down.

So that a caller can ask somebody whether to trust a certificate at
all, a handshake that fails over the certificate says which one it was:
the same digest, as `refused_cert_sha256` among the options the caller
gets back from the opening that failed. The option dictionary is what
an opening already hands back with whatever was not used.

To do either, a client now always stops when the server has shown its
certificate and evaluates the trust itself, as it did before only when
`ca_file` was given. The evaluation is the one Secure Transport makes.

